THE COMMIT ABOVE IS AN UNMERGED PULL-REQUEST HEAD, not a commit on meta's
main: V-M-Pioneer-Trading/meta#84, the branch `feat/fixture-safe-methods`,
and it is that branch's **second** head — the first review of this package
produced four more fixture cases, so the record moved from `45b13eb` to
`ea03016` and the sha256 moved with it. It is recorded as a PR head
deliberately — this package implements the safe-method rule and cannot
honestly point at a commit that predates it — but it is a temporary state.
**When meta#84 merges, re-point this record at the squash commit on main.**
The bytes do not change when it does: main's squash commit carries the same
blob, so `blob`, `bytes` and `sha256` above stay exactly as they are and only
the `meta commit` line moves. If that re-point ever changes the sha256, the
two are not the same fixture and something was edited during the merge.

The sha256 is over the LF bytes of the blob as git stores it, which is what
`git show <commit>:fixtures/introspection.json` prints. test/conformance.test.ts
recomputes it on every run and fails if the vendored copy and this record ever
disagree, so a hand-edit of the copy is a red test rather than silent drift.

To re-vendor after meta changes the fixture:

  git -C ../meta show HEAD:fixtures/introspection.json > test/fixtures/introspection.json
  node -e "const c=require('crypto'),f=require('fs');const b=f.readFileSync('test/fixtures/introspection.json');console.log(b.length,c.createHash('sha256').update(b).digest('hex'))"

then update the commit, date, version, bytes and sha256 above. Change meta
first; a copy that leads the original is drift pointing the other way.

The conformance suite also asserts the sorted list of case names, so a case
added upstream fails here until it is implemented rather than being skipped.
